+---
+name: data-understanding
+description: REQUIRED before analysis of an uploaded, ERP, WFERP, or otherwise unfamiliar dataset. Use for data profiling, schema/ontology inspection, descriptive or diagnostic analysis, data quality review, choosing whether ML is appropriate, and any request to understand a dataset before prediction.
+---
+
+# Ontology-guided data understanding
+
+This skill is data-first and evidence-bound. It does not prescribe a fixed chart list, analysis DAG, model, or report layout. Tool schemas, authorized refs, the approved ontology, and the user's current intent are authoritative.
+
+## Non-negotiable rules
+
+- Use only real tool results from the current session. Never use mock, fixture, fabricated, guessed, copied, or fallback data.
+- Keep the original result grain and row count. Do not sample, truncate, down-select rows, or create a derived dataset for profiling or ML. Chart bins and time buckets are visual-only aggregations.
+- Dataset IDs, field names, date semantics, relations, query refs, and ontology hashes must come from inspect/classify/plan evidence. Never translate, rename, concatenate, or hardcode physical field names.
+- An uploaded ontology is an observed candidate. Say `inferred` or `observed` unless an approved ontology snapshot explicitly says `approved`; never promote a candidate by wording.
+- Before any query or sandbox execution, show an Analysis Preview containing the dataset, grain/date meaning, exact fields, row bounds, ontology status, and intended operation. Wait for user confirmation.
+- Do not call ML tools merely because numeric columns exist. Without explicit predictive intent, default to ontology-guided profiling and descriptive/diagnostic/comparative analysis. Ask which decision the user wants if intent is ambiguous.
+- Recovery is native LLM reasoning: read the complete error, current tool schema, opaque refs, and contract/evidence; repair only what the evidence requires and retry. Do not invent IDs/fields, fill missing values with guesses, switch to a mock/fallback, or add a fixed retry state machine.
+
+## Data-first sequence (adapt to intent, not a fixed DAG)
+
+1. Discover or inspect the authorized dataset. Reuse the returned opaque `dataset_metadata_ref`, dataset ID, datasource identity, fields, row hint, and date range. For an upload, use its session-owned ID and the observed field classification; do not search only organization-approved ontology.
+2. For WFERP/ERP, call `search_wferp_schema` first, then author one bounded legacy-compatible `SELECT` through `plan_wferp_query`. Verify joins do not multiply grain, keys are not silently lost, and date semantics distinguish document date, posting date, production date, and event date. Never execute SQL directly.
+3. Ask the user to confirm the exact preview. The confirmation authorizes this analysis only; it does not approve ontology promotion or dashboard publication.
+4. Call the authorized Planner with exact physical fields and bounded full-data row requirements. Then call Grafana Query. The query must return the complete authorized population at its declared grain; stop if validation reports missing fields, row loss, truncation, or an unexpected grain.
+5. For a non-ML understanding request, call `profile_dataset(frame_ref)` rather than writing model-authored Python. The deterministic profile covers every row and every returned field and returns bounded facts plus visual-only assets for distributions, missingness, relationships, and any time trend supported by the data. It never chooses an ML method.
+6. Read the profile manifest, facts, warnings, ontology status, and all returned asset metadata. Decide the next action from the user's question and evidence: descriptive/WFERP analysis, a separate authorized statistical analysis, or an ML preview. Do not silently escalate from profile to prediction.
+
+## Profile interpretation
+
+- Report row/field coverage and whether `full_data=true`, `sampling=false`, and `derived_dataset=false`.
+- Distinguish missingness, low variance, type inference, temporal ordering, categorical concentration, and association. Association/correlation is not causation.
+- Treat visual grouping, histogram bins, and time aggregation as presentation only. Do not use them as a training or query input.
+- If a field has unknown or inferred semantics, state that limitation and ask for ontology confirmation instead of asserting a role.
+- For a profile dashboard, use `prepare_ml_report`, `inspect_report_artifacts`, and `compose_ml_dashboard` as generic report tools: inspect all bounded facts and all artifact/view specs (and PNGs when vision is available), produce one cross-artifact synthesis with independent per-view narratives, then resolve opaque bindings before the sole approved Grafana writer. Do not author asset URLs, raw figure data, datasource targets, or scripts.
+
+## Moving to ML
+
+Only after the user explicitly asks for prediction, estimation, forecasting, or model comparison:
+
+- Select the ML/statistics skill and create an ontology-pinned analysis contract from the observed/approved evidence. Preserve the full-data invariant.
+- Explain target, features, excluded/leakage/proxy fields, grain, split/date semantics, preprocessing fit scope, and baseline before execution. `Timestamp` or another time field may define chronological order but must not enter model features unless the approved contract explicitly allows it.
+- Keep Dummy as the baseline gate. If it wins, report that result and data-quality/feature recommendations; do not fabricate candidate settings or optimization bounds.
+- For WFERP with no explicit predictive intent, remain descriptive/diagnostic/comparative; do not force a model.
+
+## Dashboard/report safety
+
+A dashboard is a presentation of completed evidence, not a substitute for profiling. A Preview still requires explicit write intent. For a report, the LLM may choose sections, ordering, titles, collapsed state, and relevant views dynamically, but every numeric claim must reference a deterministic fact and every selected view needs its own data observation, visual observation (or `null` for spec-only), interpretation, limitation, next step, and evidence. The compositor/validator is authoritative; if it rejects a synthesis, repair the synthesis from its exact error without rerunning successful work. `compose_ml_dashboard` returns an opaque `refs.dashboard_ref`; pass it to the sole writer as `{"dashboard": {"$dashboard_ref": "<refs.dashboard_ref>"}}` instead of copying the full dashboard JSON.
+The `synthesis` object must contain exactly `format`, `report_title`, `thesis`, `thesis_evidence`, and `sections`; never include legacy top-level `purpose`, `title`, or `uid` inside it. Pass the new unique `uid` and dashboard `title` as separate `compose_ml_dashboard` arguments. Each section must follow the current tool schema; when validation reports missing or unexpected fields, repair only those fields and do not rerun completed analysis.
